---
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getItemizedSites, siteBestPower } from "../lib/sites";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

// London's whole mapped build-out (running + proposed), computed live so the
// comparison can't drift. Excludes campus overlays and closed sites.
const IN_SCOPE = new Set(["operating", "under_construction", "consented", "pipeline"]);
let londonMw = 0;
for (const f of getItemizedSites()) {
  const best = siteBestPower(f.properties);
  const r = f.properties.operational_reality;
  if (best && r && IN_SCOPE.has(r)) londonMw += best.quantity.value;
}
const londonGw = (londonMw / 1000).toFixed(1);

/**
 * Scale comparison — cluster vs cluster, but still two different measurements.
 * London: total STATED build-out on this map (running + proposed).
 * Loudoun: MEASURED county-wide data-centre power consumption (2025).
 */
const COMPARE = [
  {
    id: "london",
    label: "London, all mapped sites",
    detail: `≈${londonGw} GW stated (running + proposed)`,
    barLabel: `${londonGw} GW`,
    mw: londonMw,
    colour: "#0072B2",
    year: "2026 map",
  },
  {
    id: "loudoun",
    label: "Loudoun County, Virginia",
    detail: "5.33 GW measured cluster load (2025)",
    barLabel: "5.33 GW",
    mw: 5330,
    colour: "#E69F00",
    year: "2025",
  },
];
const compareMax = Math.max(...COMPARE.map((c) => c.mw));
const pct = (v: number, of: number) => (of > 0 ? (v / of) * 100 : 0);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Context"
  description="How London data centres press on power, water, jobs, noise, and land: UK evidence, US precedents, mitigations, and a like-for-like scale note."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl m-0 tracking-tight">Context</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--muted)] mt-2">
      The map plots sites and sourced figures. This page sets out the pressures those sites put on grids and
      communities, what US regions already show at larger scale, and mitigations already appearing in London
      dossiers. Benefits sit next to costs. Every claim below is linked; if we cannot source it, we cut it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted)] mt-3">
      Stance matches the rest of the site: disclosed, not neutral. See
      <a href={`${base}/methodology`}>methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- 1. Power -->
    <section class="mt-12" id="power">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Power and the grid</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Data centres take large, steady electricity loads. Where the local grid is already tight, new
        connections for housing and other uses can wait years.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>UK.</strong> In 2022 the Greater London Authority was alerted that parts of West London
        (Ealing, Hillingdon and Hounslow) faced electricity capacity constraints. Investigations linked the
        queue partly to rapid data-centre growth along the M4 corridor; some applicants were quoted
        connection dates into the 2030s (including figures up to about 2035–2037 in GLA materials). There
        was no formal ban on housing. Short-term fixes and connection reforms have since unlocked many
        homes, while transmission upgrades remain necessary for the long term.
        (<a href="https://www.london.gov.uk/programmes-strategies/better-infrastructure/infrastructure-coordination/development-service/west-london-electricity-capacity-constraints">GLA West London constraints</a>;
        <a href="https://www.london.gov.uk/sites/default/files/2025-12/Planning%20Regeneration%20Committee%20-%20Energy%20Infrastructure%20Report%20FINAL.pdf">GLA Planning &amp; Regeneration Committee, Dec 2025</a>.)
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>US.</strong> Northern Virginia’s “Data Center Alley” is the world’s densest cluster. Loudoun
        County alone reported about <strong>5.33 GW</strong> of data-centre power consumption in 2025, up
        from about 2.0 GW in 2021. Dominion Energy has reported tens of gigawatts of data-centre capacity
        under contract across its Virginia service area. Residents and regulators have argued over how much
        of rising bills and grid spend is driven by that load; Dominion disputes a simple causal story, and
        a new large-load rate class (GS-5) is due from 2027 to shift more cost onto big users.
        (<a href="https://www.loudoun.gov/DocumentCenter/View/217605/Loudoun-County-Data-Center-Brief---Turner">Loudoun County brief, 2025</a>;
        <a href="https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/dominion-energy-nearly-doubles-data-center-capacity-under-contract-to-40gw/">DCD on Dominion contract pipeline, 2025</a>;
        <a href="https://www.loudounnow.com/news/scc-approves-new-data-center-rate-class-for-dominion/article_02d5fa0c-1ed8-4771-b57d-c24765739854.html">Loudoun Now on GS-5, 2025</a>.)
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>London on this map.</strong> The west corridor (Slough–Heathrow) still holds most operating
        megawatts; large proposed schemes push north and east of the M25. See
        <a href={`${base}/pipeline`}>pipeline</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
        <strong>Mitigations on record here:</strong> grid reinforcement and flexible connection products
        (the GLA/SSEN work above), and
        <a href={`${base}/site/manor-farm-slough`}>Manor Farm</a>’s consented ~100 MW battery energy storage
        alongside the data centre.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Water -->
    <section class="mt-12" id="water">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Water</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Cooling can use large volumes of potable water. UK operators are
        <strong>not required to disclose</strong> water use, and data centres are excluded from the
        Environment Agency’s National Framework for Water Resources, so most dossiers here show water as
        not disclosed, not as zero. Full method note:
        <a href={`${base}/methodology#water`}>methodology § water</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>UK.</strong> An April 2026 Global Action Plan analysis reported that about
        <strong>84%</strong> of proposed UK data centres sit in areas already water-stressed or projected
        to be by 2040
        (<a href="https://www.watermagazine.co.uk/2026/04/27/84-of-proposed-uk-data-centres-to-be-built-in-water-stressed-areas/">Water Magazine</a>).
        Water UK has told Parliament that some proposed centres in the Affinity Water region have requested
        up to about <strong>three million litres a day</strong>, and that the sector’s demand is not built
        into national water planning
        (<a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/21/not-enough-water-for-uks-datacentre-plans-trade-body-says">Guardian / Water UK, Jul 2026</a>).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>US.</strong> In The Dalles, Oregon, a public-records fight over Google’s data-centre water
        use ended in a 2022 settlement that forced disclosure; later reporting showed Google taking a large
        share of city water as campuses expanded
        (<a href="https://www.oregonlive.com/silicon-forest/2022/12/the-dalles-settles-public-records-lawsuit-over-googles-data-centers-will-disclose-water-use.html">Oregonian, Dec 2022</a>;
        <a href="https://oregoncapitalchronicle.com/2026/01/19/as-googles-water-demands-grow-the-dalles-aims-to-pull-more-from-mount-hood-forest/">Oregon Capital Chronicle, Jan 2026</a>).
        Drought-constrained states such as Arizona show the same conflict pathway when cooling competes with
        other users. Treat these as precedents for politics and disclosure, not as identical hydrology to the
        Thames basin.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
        <strong>Mitigations on record here:</strong> operator claims of air or closed-loop cooling with low
        on-site water at
        <a href={`${base}/site/ada-docklands`}>Ada Docklands</a>,
        <a href={`${base}/site/google-waltham-cross`}>Google Waltham Cross</a>, and
        <a href={`${base}/site/vantage-lhr1-powergate`}>Vantage</a>, all flagged as claims until metered.
        Ada and Google also state heat-reuse or district-heat provisions, which we show as claims.
        “Waterless” on site still shifts water to the power station through higher electricity use
        (<a href={`${base}/methodology#water`}>methodology</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Jobs -->
    <section class="mt-12" id="jobs">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Jobs and the local economy</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Promoters emphasise capital investment, business rates, and construction employment. Permanent
        on-site staffing is usually small relative to the land and power take.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>Example on this map.</strong> Manor Farm’s economic statement and inquiry materials put
        construction at about <strong>430–490</strong> full-time-equivalent jobs over the build, and
        operations at about <strong>65</strong> FTE once running
        (<a href="https://www.slough.gov.uk/downloads/file/4995/cd-1-39-economic-statement">Economic Statement</a>;
        <a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckg0717xr3wo">BBC</a>).
        Those are applicant figures in the consent trail, not independent audits.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>Benefit side.</strong> The same facilities underpin cloud and AI services used across the
        wider economy. That benefit is real. It is also spread thin: the rates and construction jobs stay
        local, but most of the digital gain is used nationally. We do not score “net public value” on this
        site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. Noise -->
    <section class="mt-12" id="noise">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Noise and health</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Continuous cooling plant and periodic diesel (or other) generator testing are among the impacts
        residents cite most often in objections. Emissions from testing add an air-quality concern beside
        noise.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>US.</strong> Suburban complaints in Northern Virginia and places such as Chandler, Arizona,
        have turned on overnight noise and generator exercises. The pattern is useful: once campuses sit
        beside housing, acoustic conditions become a planning lever.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
        <strong>Mitigations:</strong> planning conditions on plant enclosure, hours for generator testing,
        acoustic barriers, and (where proposed) HVO or electric backup. Check each dossier’s planning
        documents rather than assuming a London-wide standard.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Land -->
    <section class="mt-12" id="land">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Land</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        London’s build-out mixes brownfield industrial reuse (Docklands, Trading Estate halls) with
        green-belt and farmland schemes on the fringe:
        <a href={`${base}/site/manor-farm-slough`}>Manor Farm</a>,
        <a href={`${base}/site/equinix-south-mimms`}>Equinix South Mimms</a>, and
        <a href={`${base}/site/digital-reef-east-havering`}>Digital Reef East Havering</a>.
        Brownfield-first is the usual mitigation in policy language; green-belt loss is decided case by
        case, often at appeal or by the Secretary of State.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3" id="land-question">
        <strong>The land question.</strong> Land is finite, and each consent decides what a parcel serves
        once it changes hands. A scheme can convert green belt or working farmland into a fenced,
        single-use facility that takes large power and water while employing few people locally: Manor
        Farm’s own figures put operations at about 65 FTE (<a href="#jobs">jobs</a>). On any planning
        horizon that change is hard to reverse; a hall of racks and cooling plant does not go back to being
        a field.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3" id="rival-claims">
        <strong>Rival claims on scarce capacity.</strong> The pressure is not only about the ground. Land,
        grid headroom, and water are shared resources, and a data centre’s claim on each one competes with
        other uses. In West London, data-centre grid demand sat in the same constrained queue that delayed
        new housing connections (<a href="#power">power</a>). Green belt does work while it stays open,
        checking sprawl and holding land in reserve, and a consent spends that.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3" id="who-carries-the-cost">
        <strong>Who carries the cost.</strong> The local costs concentrate: the land itself, the noise, the
        traffic, and sometimes water use fall on the immediate area. Most of the benefit is national: the
        cloud and AI capacity these sites host is used across the economy, not mainly nearby
        (<a href="#jobs">jobs</a>). Opponents call this a land-justice problem.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        <strong>Benefits.</strong> Clustering compute near existing hubs and fibre can lower running costs,
        and building on hard-to-redevelop industrial land puts derelict sites back to work. On this map the
        brownfield conversions (Docklands, the Trading Estate halls) are where that case is strongest:
        industrial reuse that raises no green-belt question at all.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Whether a given scheme is a good use of its land isn’t this site’s call: each dossier just logs the
        prior use of the plot and who signed off.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. London vs US -->
    <section class="mt-12" id="london-vs-us">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">How London compares with the US</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Single US AI and cloud campuses are often quoted in the <strong>multi-gigawatt</strong> range, and
        Loudoun County’s measured cluster load alone was about <strong>5.33 GW in 2025</strong>. London’s
        entire mapped build-out, running plus proposed, is about <strong>{londonGw} GW</strong> stated; its
        largest single scheme is about <strong>600 MW</strong> (Digital Reef East Havering). London is
        smaller per site and far more constrained on land, water, and grid headroom, so friction such as
        connection delays, water stress, and green-belt fights shows up at lower absolute megawatts. Some of
        it is already here.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4" role="img"
        aria-label={`Scale comparison: London's whole mapped build-out about ${londonGw} gigawatts stated; Loudoun County 2025 measured cluster load 5.33 gigawatts.`}>
        {COMPARE.map((row) => (
          <div>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 text-sm">
              <p class="m-0 font-medium">{row.label}</p>
              <p class="m-0 font-tabular text-[var(--muted)]">
                {row.barLabel}<span class="text-xs"> · {row.year}</span>
              </p>
            </div>
            <p class="m-0 mt-0.5 text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">{row.detail}</p>
            <div class="mt-2 h-8 rounded overflow-hidden bg-[var(--line)]">
              <div
                class="h-full flex items-center px-2 text-xs font-medium text-white whitespace-nowrap"
                style={`width:${Math.max(pct(row.mw, compareMax), 12)}%;background:${row.colour}`}
              >
                {row.barLabel}
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
        Metric caution: both sides are cluster-scale, but they are not the same measurement. London’s figure
        is the stated total of every mapped site, running and proposed. Loudoun’s is measured power actually
        consumed across the county cluster in 2025. Read it as scale, not a like-for-like league table.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 border-t border-[var(--line)] pt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Use the map</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
        <a href={`${base}/`}>Map</a>
        · <a href={`${base}/pipeline`}>Pipeline</a>
        · <a href={`${base}/nearby`}>Near you</a>
        · <a href={`${base}/research`}>Research</a>
        · <a href={`${base}/methodology`}>Methodology</a>
        · <a href={`${base}/data/sites.geojson`}>GeoJSON</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
